/*!
\ingroup qtopenapi-examples
\example oauth2client
\examplecategory {Networking}
\meta tags {widgets, network, http, openapi, oauth2}
\title OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow Client
\brief Example of how to authorize a generated OpenAPI client with
Qt Network Authorization.
\image {oauth2client.webp}
       {Window with Log out and Get profile buttons and a log of the
       authorization requests}

Generated Qt OpenAPI clients do not implement OAuth 2.0 themselves. When the
specification protects an operation with a security scheme of type
\c oauth2, the generated API only expects an access token, which it sends as a
\c Bearer token. Obtaining and refreshing that token is up to the application.

This example uses \l{Qt Network Authorization} for this purpose. It generates a
client from \c oauth2client.yaml, runs the
\l{OAuth 2.0 Overview#Authorization code flow}{authorization code flow} with
\l QOAuth2AuthorizationCodeFlow and then requests the profile of the
logged-in user.

So that the example runs without an account or a client registration, it
ships its own small authorization and resource server, written in Go. The
API routes of the server are generated from the same specification with the
\c go-gin-server generator of the upstream OpenAPI Generator. The application
starts the server automatically.

\include examples-run.qdocinc

\note Building the demo server requires \l{https://go.dev}{Go}. If Go is not
      found when the example is configured, the application is still built,
      but you have to start the server yourself with \c {go run .} in the
      \c server directory before pressing \uicontrol {Log in}.

\section1 Application functionality

The application has two buttons and a log window. \uicontrol {Log in} opens
the consent page of the demo authorization server in the system browser.
After you select \uicontrol {Allow}, the browser is redirected to the local
reply handler, and the flow exchanges the authorization code for tokens.
\uicontrol {Log in} then changes to \uicontrol {Log out}, which removes the
tokens again. \uicontrol {Get profile} calls the generated
\c getCurrentUserProfile() operation and prints the result to the log. It is
always enabled, so you can also see how the server rejects a request without
a valid token. The log also shows the requests that the server receives,
including the automatic token refresh that happens about every 30 seconds.

\section1 The security scheme in the specification

The specification declares the OAuth 2.0 endpoints and the scopes that the
client needs. The application uses these values to configure
\l QOAuth2AuthorizationCodeFlow:

\quotefromfile oauth2client/oauth2client.yaml
\skipto securitySchemes:
\printuntil email:read:

\c refreshUrl has no counterpart in Qt Network Authorization. Refresh requests
are always sent to \c tokenUrl. As is common, this specification uses the
same URL for both. The URLs use plain \c http because the demo
server only listens on the loopback interface. A real service must use
\c https. See \l{Qt OpenAPI Security Considerations}.

\section1 Generating the client and linking Qt Network Authorization

The client library is generated from the specification at configure time with
\l qt_add_openapi_client(). The application additionally links against
\c Qt6::NetworkAuth:

\snippet oauth2client/CMakeLists.txt find-package
\snippet oauth2client/CMakeLists.txt generate-client
\snippet oauth2client/CMakeLists.txt link

\section1 Configuring the authorization code flow

The \c ProfileClient class owns the flow, the reply handler and the generated
\c UsersApi:

\snippet oauth2client/profileclient.h class

The constructor sets the endpoints, the scopes, and the client ID on the
flow and enables automatic token refresh. The demo server accepts only one
client ID, \c qt-oauth2-example. Client IDs of native applications are public
by design, so the example hardcodes it:

\snippet oauth2client/profileclient.cpp oauth-configuration

The reply handler listens on the loopback interface on a free port. The demo
server accepts any loopback redirect URI, whereas a real authorization server
usually requires the exact URI to be registered in advance.
For other ways to receive the redirect, such as a private-use URI scheme on
mobile platforms, see \l{OAuth 2.0 Overview#Choosing a reply handler}
{Choosing a reply handler}.

\section1 Handing the token to the generated client

The generated client stores a single bearer token. The example connects the
\l{QAbstractOAuth::}{tokenChanged()} signal to \c setBearerToken(), so the
client always uses the latest token, whether it came from the initial grant or
from an automatic refresh:

\snippet oauth2client/profileclient.cpp token-handover

To complete the authorization, the user must log in on the authorization
page. The flow emits \l{QAbstractOAuth::}{authorizeWithBrowser()} with the URL
of that page, and the example opens it in the system browser with
\l QDesktopServices::openUrl().

When the flow returns to the \c NotAuthenticated status (for example, because
a refresh failed), or when the server responds with HTTP status 401, the
example removes the token from the generated client, and the user can log in
again.

The example logs failures from two signals: \l{QAbstractOAuth::}{requestFailed()}
for any failed request, and \l{QAbstractOAuth2::}{serverReportedErrorOccurred()}
for errors that the authorization server reports explicitly:

\snippet oauth2client/profileclient.cpp oauth-errors

\section1 Calling the API

Once the application is authorized, calling the generated operation works the
same as for any other Qt OpenAPI client. The token is attached automatically:

\snippet oauth2client/profileclient.cpp api-call

\section1 The demo server

The \c server directory contains the source code of the demo server. Its API
routes are generated from \c oauth2client.yaml, and the OAuth 2.0 endpoints
are implemented by hand in \c server/go/oauth_server.go. The server is meant
only for running this example and is not a reference implementation of an
authorization server.

\section1 Security considerations

The access token and the refresh token are as valuable as the user's account.
The example keeps them in memory only. An application that stores the refresh
token to log in again without user interaction must protect it accordingly.
See \l{Qt OpenAPI Security Considerations} and
\l{Qt Network Authorization Security Considerations} for details.

\sa {All Qt Examples}, {ChatGPT RESTful API Client}
*/
